title: "Sparse factor model for P"
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Sparse factor model for P}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "figures/factor-phenotypic-covariance-",
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5, dpi = 80,
                      out.width = "100%")
```

The 35 log-transformed mouse traits are dominated by a single size gradient:
the first principal component takes about 40% of the variance and the
eigenvalues then fall away quickly. That is the low-rank structure a factor
model describes, so `lm_phen_cov(covariance_structure = "factor")` estimates `P`
as a few sparse factors plus trait-specific variances instead of a full 35 by 35
correlation matrix.

The factors stay on the observed log-trait scale on purpose. Rotating the traits
first (size plus shape scores, see `size_shape()`) spreads every loading over
all 35 traits, which destroys the sparsity the prior is there to find and moves
the low-rank structure out of `P` and into the rotation itself.

```{r packages, message = FALSE}
library(stanimal)
```

```{r data}
traits <- names(ratones_morpho)[12:46]
Y <- log(as.matrix(ratones_morpho[, traits]))
X <- stats::model.matrix(~ SEX + AGE + LIN, data = ratones_morpho)
dim(Y)
round(pc1_variance_share(Y), 3)
```

## How many factors?

The scree says where the low-rank structure is, and parallel analysis says how
many dimensions clear the noise floor. That count is an upper bound, not a
target.

```{r n-factors}
set.seed(1)
suggested <- suggest_n_factors(Y, X)
c(dimensions = as.integer(suggested), variance_share = round(attr(suggested, "share"), 2))
round(scree_eigenvalues(Y, X)[1:8, ], 3)
```

```{r scree, fig.height = 4}
plot(scree_eigenvalues(Y, X))
```

Parallel analysis keeps `r as.integer(suggested)` dimensions, but the first two
stand clear of a flat tail and the last ones only just clear their cutoffs. A
three-factor fit gives bimodal communalities (`Rhat` 1.5 across four chains),
which is the model saying that direction is not in the data, so two factors it
is. The column decay is stronger than the 0.8 default because the two columns
come out at similar sizes: at 0.8 their labels swap between chains, and at 0.5
they are ordered and converge.

## Fit

```{r fit, message = FALSE}
fit <- lm_phen_cov(
  Y, X,
  covariance_structure = "factor", n_factors = 2,
  hs_global_scale = 2, hs_slab_scale = 2,
  column_decay = 0.5,
  iter = 2000, warmup = 1000, chains = 4, cores = 4,
  refresh = 0, show_messages = FALSE, seed = 1
)
fit$diagnostic_summary()
```

## Against the least-squares residual covariance

`P` is on the log-trait scale, so it is directly comparable with the covariance
of the least-squares residuals. A factor model is a rank-`k` approximation, so
its error has to be read against the best a rank-`k` structure can do, not
against zero. The errors are on the correlation scale, because an absolute error
on variances of about 0.001 says nothing.

```{r compare}
P_hat <- draws_mean(fit, "P")
P_ols <- stats::cov(stats::lm.fit(X, Y)$residuals)
corr_hat <- stats::cov2cor(P_hat)
corr_ols <- stats::cov2cor(P_ols)
relative_error <- function(estimate, target)
  sqrt(mean((estimate - target)^2) / mean(target^2))

eigen_ols <- eigen(corr_ols, symmetric = TRUE)
best_rank <- eigen_ols$vectors[, 1:2] %*% diag(eigen_ols$values[1:2]) %*%
  t(eigen_ols$vectors[, 1:2])
diag(best_rank) <- 1
c(factor_model = relative_error(corr_hat, corr_ols),
  best_rank_2 = relative_error(best_rank, corr_ols))
round(rbind(factor = diag(P_hat)[1:5], ols = diag(P_ols)[1:5]), 4)
```

The horseshoe shrinks, so the fitted correlations should sit slightly closer to
zero than the least-squares ones. A large gap the other way would mean the prior
is inflating the common structure instead of smoothing it.

```{r shrinkage}
c(factor_model = mean(abs(corr_hat[upper.tri(corr_hat)])),
  ols = mean(abs(corr_ols[upper.tri(corr_ols)])))
```

## Loadings, communality and uniqueness

A factor's sign is unidentified: flipping one loading column leaves `P`
unchanged, so a chain can visit both orientations, and averaging them would
cancel the factor out of the posterior mean. `draws_mean()` aligns every draw to
the trait with the largest mean absolute loading first, so nothing needs to be
done here.

Communality is the share of a trait's variance taken by the common factors, and
uniqueness is its complement. Both are invariant to rotation; loadings and
factor magnitudes are not, so read those only after `P` and communality have
converged.

```{r loadings}
lambda <- draws_mean(fit, "loadings")
round(lambda[1:5, ], 3)
fit$summary("factor_magnitude")[, c("variable", "mean", "sd", "rhat", "ess_bulk")]
fit$summary("communality")[1:5, c("variable", "mean", "sd", "rhat", "ess_bulk")]
sum(draws_mean(fit, "communality"))   # total common variance
```

```{r heatmaps, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 8}
plot_corr(corr_hat, "Factor P: posterior mean correlations")
plot_corr(corr_ols, "Least-squares residual correlations")
```

```{r loading-heatmap, fig.width = 4, fig.height = 8}
plot_corr(lambda, "Posterior mean loadings")
```
